    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The joint prior distribution over all coefficients is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$\\prod_{j=1}^{p} P(\\beta_j) = \\prod_{j=1}^{p} \\frac{1}{2b} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{|\\beta_j|}{b}\\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "$= \\left(\\frac{1}{2b}\\right)^p \\exp\\left(-\\frac{1}{b}\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j|\\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "Plugging this into the posterior distribution, we get:\n",
        "\n",
        "$\\mathcal{L}(Y | X, \\beta) P(\\beta) = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\sigma}\\right)^n \\exp\\left(-\\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\epsilon_i^2\\right) * \\left(\\frac{1}{2b}\\right)^p \\exp\\left(-\\frac{1}{b}\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j|\\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "= $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\sigma}\\right)^n \\left(\\frac{1}{2b}\\right)^p \\exp\\left(-\\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\epsilon_i^2 - \\frac{1}{b}\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j|\\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "Now to find the maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimate of $\\beta$, we take the log of the posterior distribution:\n",
        "\n",
        "$\\underbrace{\\underbrace{nlog(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\sigma}) + plog(\\frac{1}{2b})}_{Constant} - \\underbrace{(\\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\epsilon_i^2 + \\frac{1}{b}\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j|)}_{Minimize this}}_{In\\ order\\ to\\ maximize\\ this}$\n",
        "\n",
        "Now the problem becomes:\n",
        "\n",
        "$arg\\min_{\\beta} \\left( \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\epsilon_i^2 + \\frac{1}{b}\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j| \\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "$=arg\\min_{\\beta} \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2}\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\epsilon_i^2 + \\frac{2\\sigma^2}{b}\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j| \\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since both $\\sigma$ and $b$ are constants, we can ignore the $\\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2}$ term outside the brackets and use a new constant $\\lambda$ to replace the term: $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\sigma^2}{b}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Now we have:\n",
        "\n",
        "$arg\\min_{\\beta} \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\epsilon_i^2 + \\lambda\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j| \\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "$=arg\\min_{\\beta} \\left( RSS + \\lambda\\sum_{j=1}^{p} |\\beta_j| \\right)$\n",
        "\n",
        "which is exactly the Lasso regularization formulation, with $\\lambda = \\frac{2\\sigma^2}{b}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, the Lasso solutions are actually the maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimates of the coefficients under a Laplace prior distribution. \n",
        "\n",
        "As we can see from Figure.2, the smaller $b$ gets, or equivamently, the larger $\\lambda$ gets, the distribution gets a sharper peak at 0. In other words, the strongger the regularization parameter gets, the more the coefficients get pushed towards zero, encouraging sparsity in the coefficients.\n",
        " \n",
        "### Ridge\n",
        "\n",
        "Ridge regularization corresponds to a Gaussian prior distribution for the coefficients:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(\\beta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\tau} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\beta^2}{2\\tau^2}\\right)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\tau^2$ is the variance parameter."
      ],
